#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055850} :activity/id #uuid "b109ea08-5fe3-5152-adbd-3c69b5b8ee22" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056039 :tutorial/content "Some sets are so important that special symbols are used to represent them. \n\n- The set $\\mathbb{N}$ represents the set of **natural numbers.** This set contains all the positive whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\Bbb{N} = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, ... \\}\n$$\n\nIt's essential to remember that the natural numbers *do not contain zero!* However, if we wish to refer to the set of natural numbers *and* zero, we use the symbol $\\mathbb N_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\Bbb{N}_0 = \\{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,... \\}\n$$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}$ represents the set of **integers.** This set contains the positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n\\Bbb{Z} = \\left\\{... , -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, ... \\right\\}\n$$\n\nThe use of the letter \"Z\" comes from the German word \"Zahlen,\" which means \"numbers\".\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}$ represents the set of **rational numbers**. This set contains all fractions that can be expressed as the ratio (or **Q**uotient) of two integers.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}$ represents the set of **real numbers**. This set contains all possible rational and irrational numbers (such as $\\pi, \\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{3}, \\ldots$).\n\n- The set $\\mathbb C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**. These consist of any number of the form $a+\\textrm i b,$ where $a,b\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\nWe can think of these special sets as being arranged in a hierarchy, as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/59/599a73b11c35e993dd0c8b202483229f14d8d085bc4fc783c59fa54a90ae1347.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "2d61ed1f-938f-51f3-8f72-19f2a9db35ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8214 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d4c7653a-5716-5862-8c22-fcb9bf1b8787" :step/math-academy-id 63830 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055851}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054429 :question/id #uuid "e557c534-7392-5c31-9e08-43347736716e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13138" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb N, \\quad \\mathbb Z, \\quad \\mathbb Q, \\quad \\mathbb R, \\quad \\mathbb C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $3.25$ belong?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Q}$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following: \n\n- The number $3.25$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Z}$ nor $\\mathbb{N},$  so we can write\n\n$$\n3.25\\notin\\mathbb N, \\qquad 3.25\\notin\\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- However, notice that $3.25$ can be written as the ratio of two integers as follows:\n\n$$\n3.25=\\dfrac{325}{100}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $3.25$ is a rational number, and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{Q}.\n$$\n\n- Every rational is a real and complex number. Consequently, $3.25$ belongs to both $\\mathbb R$ and $\\mathbb{C},$ and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad 3.25 \\in \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbb Q, \\mathbb R,$ and $\\mathbb C$ only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3de9e532-559c-5fc1-a6ad-5fcd1ed77824" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050404}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657687} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637267}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Special Sets Containing a Given Element"} :step/id #uuid "d634226b-b2d3-5ffb-9564-84414bfd148e" :step/math-academy-id 63831 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056040 :tutorial/content "We can use subscripts and superscripts to limit the special sets to being only positive (\"$+$\" superscript), only negative (\"$-$\" superscript), and to include zero (\"$0$\" subscript).\n\nFor example, $\\mathbb{Z}^+$ represents the positive integers, while $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ represents the negative integers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{+} & =\\{1,2,3,…\\} \\\\ {Z}^{-} & =\\{-1,-2,-3,…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese sets do not include $0.$ If we want to include $0$ too, then we can use a subscript $0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}_{0}^{+} & =\\{0,1,2,3,…\\} \\\\ {Z}_{0}^{-} & =\\{0,-1,-2,-3,…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table describing all the special sets is given below.\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\emptyset$ | The empty set, $\\{\\}$ |\n| $\\mathbb{N}$ | The set of natural numbers, $\\{1,2,3,...\\}$ |\n| $\\mathbb{N}_0$ | The set of natural numbers and zero,  $\\{0,1,2,3,...\\}$ |\n| $\\mathbb{Z}$ | The set of integers, $\\{0,\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm3,...\\}$ |\n| $\\mathbb{Z}^+$ | The set of positive integers, $\\{1,2,3,...\\}.$ |\n| $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ | The set of positive integers and zero, $\\{0,1,2,3,...\\}$ |\n| $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ | The set of negative integers, $\\{-1,-2,-3,...\\}$ |\n| $\\mathbb{Z}_0^-$ | The set of negative integers and zero, $\\{0,-1,-2,-3,...\\}$ |\n| $\\mathbb{Q}$ | The set of rational numbers |\n| $\\mathbb{Q}^+$ | The set of positive rational numbers |\n| $\\mathbb{Q}_0^+$ | The set of positive rational numbers and zero |\n| $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ | The set of negative rational numbers |\n| $\\mathbb{Q}_0^-$ | The set of negative rational numbers and zero |\n| $\\mathbb{R}$ | The set of real numbers |\n| $\\mathbb{R}^+$ | The set of positive real numbers |\n| $\\mathbb{R}_0^+$ | The set of positive real numbers and zero |\n| $\\mathbb{R}^-$ | The set of negative real numbers |\n| $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ | The set of negative real numbers and zero |" :tutorial/id #uuid "05d0929b-b494-531b-9eea-35af04606555" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8215 :tutorial/title "Subscript and Superscript Notation With Special Sets"} :step/id #uuid "be67923c-81ed-5955-9d91-2085e8392133" :step/math-academy-id 63835 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055853}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054430 :question/id #uuid "ce8a56c1-709e-50e8-ac10-d3f92bf15db0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13139" :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-?$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$\n\n- $- 0.2$\n\n- $0$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$ is negative but not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $-0.2$ is negative, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n-0.2 = -\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-0.2$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $0$ is a rational number but not negative, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4} =- 2$, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt 4 = -2 = -\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-\\sqrt 4$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc6b3ee-0251-5c9c-9358-bd192c434dd1" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670584} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637314}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to Scripted Special Sets"} :step/id #uuid "b39f3c14-b001-542e-9d27-3c7c1a3a3632" :step/math-academy-id 63836 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054431 :question/id #uuid "e8b7d098-3cda-548e-b1d3-e1f2a87b59b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-13140" :question/problem "To which of the following sets do all the negative integers belong? \n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Q}^-$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$" :question/worked-solution "The negative integers are $-1, -2, -3, \\ldots$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}$ contains all the integers, including the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ contains the positive integers and $0.$ So, it does not contain the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative rational numbers. Since all integers are rational numbers, $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative real numbers and $0.$ Since all integers are real numbers, $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d132801-b9cc-5c0c-a8fa-0c8b840c05bd" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670585} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637243}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Containing a Given Element"} :step/id #uuid "ef9324d3-cb15-59de-89de-37397aedb2e2" :step/math-academy-id 63840}] :activity/title "Special Sets" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054906}]}
